number and syscalls
* https://github.com/apple/darwin-xnu/blob/main/bsd/kern/syscalls.master
  - https://stackoverflow.com/questions/48845697/macos-64-bit-system-call-table
  NUMBER  AUDIT_EVENT  PLATFORMS  { C_SIGNATURE }  { COMMENTS }

  Field       | Example                        | Meaning
  -----       | -------                        | -------
  NUMBER      | 0, 1, 12, 206                  | Raw syscall number (0–~600). In assembly/direct calls, add 0x2000000 for BSD class: 0x2000000 + NUMBER
  AUDIT_EVENT | AUE_EXIT, AUE_CHDIR, AUE_NULL  | Security audit event type. AUE_NULL = no audit logging; others log that syscall for security/compliance tracking
  PLATFORMS   | ALL                            | Which architectures/OS versions support it. ALL = x86_64, ARM64, etc. May be 64 for 64-bit only, specific OS versions, etc.
  C_SIGNATURE | int exit(int rval)             | C function prototype showing name, return type, and parameters. user_addr_t = userland pointer; NO_SYSCALL_STUB = not exported via libc wrapper
  COMMENTS    | indirect syscall, old ATsocket | Implementation notes: indirect = multiplex calls through one number; old = deprecated/obsolete

  NO_SYSCALL_STUB
  must invoke it directly via syscall assembly/number, not via libc. Most modern
  calls have wrappers in libsystem_kernel.dylib
